1
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:12,420
But Pallas Athena went her way to
wide-dancing-grounded Lacedaemon

2
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:18,740
to remind the shining son of great-hearted Odysseus
of his homecoming, and to stir him to return.

3
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:24,640
She found Telemachus and Nestor's splendid son
sleeping in the forecourt of glorious Menelaus —

4
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:27,440
Nestor's son, at least, mastered by soft sleep;

5
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:34,120
but no sweet sleep held Telemachus: through the ambrosial
night cares for his father kept his heart awake.

6
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:37,420
And gray-eyed Athena stood near him and spoke:

7
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:41,280
"Telemachus, it is no longer good,
this roaming far from home

8
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,100
with your goods abandoned, and men in your own house

9
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,760
of such overbearing insolence —
or they will devour it all,

10
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:51,440
dividing your goods among them,
and your journey come to nothing.

11
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:54,480
Rouse Menelaus of the great war-cry — quickly —

12
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,600
to send you off, so you may find
your blameless mother still at home.

13
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:03,840
For already her father and her brothers press her
to marry Eurymachus: he outbids all the suitors

14
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,280
in gifts, and keeps raising the bride-price higher.

15
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:10,480
Take care nothing is carried
from the house against your will.

16
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,880
You know what kind of heart is in a woman's breast:

17
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:16,100
she wants to swell the house of the man who marries her,

18
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,580
and of her former children,
of the wedded husband she has lost,

19
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:23,060
she thinks no more once he is dead. She asks nothing.

20
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:28,740
No — go back yourself and put everything in the hands
of whichever of the slave-women seems the best to you,

21
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:31,580
until the gods reveal to you your honored bride.

22
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:34,900
And another word I will tell you: lay it up in your heart.

23
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:38,400
The best men of the suitors lie
in wait for you — deliberately —

24
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:44,080
in the strait between Ithaca and rugged Samos,
hungry to kill you before you reach your fathers' land.

25
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,540
But I do not look for that. No:
sooner will the earth close over

26
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:50,560
more than one of the suitor-men who eat away your living.

27
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,480
Keep your well-made ship far out from the islands,

28
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,600
and sail by night as well as day:
that one of the deathless ones

29
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,660
who guards and keeps you will send a breeze behind you.

30
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:04,560
And when you reach the first shore of Ithaca,
send the ship and all the companions on to the city,

31
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:07,340
but yourself, before all else, go to the swineherd

32
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:11,140
who keeps your swine —
whose heart is loyal to you all the same.

33
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:16,300
Sleep the night there; and send him into the city
to carry the news to watchful-minded Penelope:

34
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:19,280
that you are safe, and have come home from Pylos."

35
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:21,700
So speaking, she went away to high Olympus.

36
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:27,420
And he woke Nestor's son out of his sweet sleep,
rousing him with a kick of his heel, and said to him:

37
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:31,200
"Wake up, Peisistratus, son of Nestor.
Bring out the strong-hoofed

38
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:35,000
horses and yoke them under the chariot,
so we can be on our road."

39
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,460
Then Peisistratus, Nestor's son, answered him:

40
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:40,500
"Telemachus — however we press for the road, there is no

41
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,400
driving through the pitch-dark night.
And it will soon be dawn.

42
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,920
Wait, then, until the hero Atreus' son, Menelaus

43
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:51,020
famed for the spear, brings his
gifts and sets them in the chariot

44
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:53,720
and sends us on our way with kindly words.

45
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:58,240
For a guest remembers, all his days,
the host who shows him kindness."

46
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:02,480
So he spoke; and at once Dawn
of the golden throne came on.

47
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:08,780
And Menelaus of the great war-cry came up to them,
fresh-risen from his bed, from beside fair-haired Helen.

48
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:14,000
When the dear son of Odysseus caught sight of him,
he hurried the shining tunic on about his body

49
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,940
and threw his great mantle over his sturdy shoulders —

50
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:23,720
the young hero — and went out; and coming up beside him,
Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus, said:

51
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:27,980
"Menelaus, son of Atreus,
Zeus-nurtured, leader of peoples:

52
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:33,660
send me back now, at last, to my own dear fatherland,
for now my heart is longing to go home."

53
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:36,580
Then Menelaus of the great war-cry answered him:

54
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:39,540
"Telemachus — far be it from me to hold you here for long,

55
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:45,440
when you strain for home. I would blame another man
as much for it — a host who loved beyond measure

56
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:50,320
or hated beyond measure:
due measure is better in all things.

57
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:55,940
It is equal wrong to push out a guest who does
not wish to go, and to hold one back who is eager.

58
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,840
Cherish the guest who is with you;
speed the one who would leave.

59
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:03,120
Only wait until I bring my gifts
and set them in the chariot —

60
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:07,000
beautiful gifts; your own eyes will
see them — and I tell the women

61
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,860
to lay a meal in the hall from the plenty stored within.

62
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:14,780
It is both honors at once — the glory and the profit —

63
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:18,680
to dine before you go, over the wide and boundless earth.

64
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:22,140
And if you care to swing through
Hellas and the heart of Argos,

65
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:25,120
I will go with you myself: I will yoke you horses

66
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:28,500
and guide you through the cities
of men; and no one will send us

67
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:32,240
away as we came — each will give
us one thing at least to carry:

68
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:37,540
some tripod of fine bronze, or a cauldron,
or a pair of mules, or a golden cup."

69
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:40,340
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

70
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:44,140
"Menelaus, son of Atreus,
Zeus-nurtured, leader of peoples,

71
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:50,260
I want to go home to our own place now. For when I
left I set no guard behind me over my possessions:

72
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:56,820
I fear I may perish myself, hunting my godlike father,
or some fine treasure vanish from my halls."

73
00:04:56,820 --> 00:05:01,520
And when Menelaus of the great war-cry heard it,
at once he told his wife and her slave-women

74
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:05,120
to lay a meal in the hall from the plenty stored within.

75
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,700
And Eteoneus, Boethous' son, came up to him,
just out of bed — for he lived not far away —

76
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:13,820
and Menelaus of the great war-cry bade him light the fire

77
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:17,580
and roast some of the meat. He heard, and did not disobey.

78
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:21,260
And Menelaus himself went down to
his fragrant treasure-chamber —

79
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:24,980
not alone: Helen went with him, and Megapenthes.

80
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:29,620
And when they came to where his treasures lay,
Atreus' son took up a two-handled cup,

81
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:34,920
and bade his son Megapenthes carry the mixing-bowl
of silver; and Helen stood by the chests

82
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:39,940
where her robes lay, worked all over
with figures — she had made them herself.

83
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,600
Helen lifted one of them out, shining among women:

84
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:48,660
the one loveliest in its weaving, and the amplest,
glittering like a star; it lay lowest of all the rest.

85
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:50,860
Then they went back up through
the house until they reached

86
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:54,140
Telemachus; and fair-haired Menelaus said to him:

87
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:57,440
"Telemachus — your homecoming, as your heart desires it,

88
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:00,880
may Zeus accomplish, the loud-thundering lord of Hera.

89
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:06,280
Of all the treasures lying stored in my house I
will give you the most beautiful, the most honored:

90
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:11,680
I will give you a wrought mixing-bowl —
all silver it is, and its rims are finished off in gold —

91
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:17,580
the work of Hephaestus. The hero Phaedimus gave it,
king of the Sidonians, when his house sheltered me

92
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:20,880
on my way home there. This I would send with you."

93
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:26,600
So speaking, the hero Atreus' son set the two-handled
cup in his hands; and strong Megapenthes carried in

94
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:32,760
the shining mixing-bowl of silver and set it before him;
and Helen of the lovely cheeks stood by

95
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:36,080
with the robe in her hands,
and spoke a word, and named him:

96
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:39,240
"I too have a gift for you, dear child — this:

97
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:42,180
a keepsake of Helen's hands,
against the hour of the marriage

98
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:45,780
you long for, for your bride
to wear. Until then let it lie

99
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:51,320
in your dear mother's keeping. And may you come rejoicing
to your well-built house and your own fatherland."

100
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:55,180
So speaking she set it in his hands;
and he received it gladly.

101
00:06:55,180 --> 00:06:58,160
And the hero Peisistratus took the gifts and stowed them

102
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,740
in the chariot-basket, wondering at them all in his heart.

103
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:08,080
Then fair-haired Menelaus led them into the house,
and the two of them sat down on benches and high seats.

104
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:10,700
A handmaid brought water for their hands in a pitcher,

105
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:13,660
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

106
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:17,220
for them to wash, and drew up
a polished table beside them.

107
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:21,440
The grave housekeeper brought
bread and set it before them,

108
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,360
laying out delicacies in plenty,
giving gladly of her store;

109
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:29,780
and close by, Boethous' son carved
the meat and shared the portions,

110
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:32,900
and the son of glorious Menelaus poured the wine.

111
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:36,420
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

112
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:42,360
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
then Telemachus and Nestor's splendid son

113
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:48,780
yoked the horses and mounted the inlaid chariot
and drove out of the gateway and the echoing porch.

114
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:55,260
And after them came Atreus' son, fair-haired Menelaus,
bearing honey-hearted wine in his right hand,

115
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:58,700
in a golden cup, for them to pour before they went.

116
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:02,320
He stood before the horses, and pledging them he spoke:

117
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:05,620
"Farewell, young men — and carry my greeting to Nestor,

118
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:08,860
shepherd of the people:
truly he was gentle as a father to me

119
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:12,280
while we sons of the Achaeans made war at Troy."

120
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:14,720
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

121
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:21,180
"All this, Zeus-nurtured, just as you say, we will
tell him when we come. And would that I as surely,

122
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:24,180
arriving home in Ithaca, might find Odysseus in the house,

123
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:30,880
and tell him how I came from you, having met with every
kindness, and bring home treasures many and fine."

124
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:34,480
And even as he spoke, a bird flew by on the right hand:

125
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:40,760
an eagle, carrying in its talons a huge white goose,
a tame one, out of the yard; and men and women

126
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:44,720
streamed after it, shouting.
And the eagle, closing near them,

127
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:47,940
shot off to the right,
in front of the horses. At the sight

128
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:54,480
their hearts lifted: the spirit warmed in every breast,
and among them Peisistratus, Nestor's son, began:

129
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:57,900
"Read it, Menelaus, Zeus-nurtured, leader of peoples:

130
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:01,780
is it for us two the god has
shown this sign, or for yourself?"

131
00:09:01,780 --> 00:09:07,600
So he spoke; and Menelaus, dear to Ares,
pondered how he might read it rightly and reply.

132
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:10,580
But long-robed Helen was before him, and spoke out:

133
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:16,380
"Hear me — and I will prophesy as the immortals cast
it in my heart, and as I think it will be accomplished.

134
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:19,380
As this eagle snatched the goose fattened in the house,

135
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:22,840
coming out of the mountain where
his kin are, and his birthplace,

136
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:26,280
so Odysseus, after much suffering and much wandering,

137
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:33,380
will come home and take his vengeance — or even now he
is home already, planting evil for all the suitors."

138
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:35,820
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

139
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:39,660
"May Zeus grant it so, the loud-thundering lord of Hera:

140
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:42,700
then even there I would pray to you as to a god."

141
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:46,360
He spoke, and laid the whip to the
horses; and they, full of fire,

142
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:49,160
sprang quickly through the city toward the plain.

143
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:52,120
All day long they shook the yoke they bore between them.

144
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:58,100
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark,
and they came to Pherae, to the house of Diocles,

145
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:00,900
son of Ortilochus, whom Alpheus fathered.

146
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:04,100
There they slept the night,
and he set guest-gifts before them.

147
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:07,780
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

148
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:14,040
they yoked the horses and mounted the inlaid chariot
and drove out of the gateway and the echoing porch;

149
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:18,840
and he whipped them to a run,
and the pair flew on, nothing loath.

150
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:21,960
And soon they came to the steep citadel of Pylos.

151
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:24,720
Then Telemachus spoke to the son of Nestor:

152
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,840
"Son of Nestor — will you
undertake a thing for me and see it

153
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:32,580
done, as I ask? Guest-friends
from of old we may call ourselves,

154
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:36,260
from our fathers' friendship —
and we are of one age besides,

155
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:39,200
and this journey has set us still more of one mind.

156
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:42,980
Do not drive me past my ship,
Zeus-nurtured: leave me there —

157
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:49,380
where the old man will keep me in his house against my will,
aching to host me. And I must get home quickly."

158
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:55,580
So he spoke; and Nestor's son debated with his heart
how he might undertake it rightly, and see it done.

159
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:59,700
And as he weighed it, this seemed to him the better way:

160
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:04,080
he turned the horses toward the
fast ship and the shore of the sea,

161
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:10,080
and in the ship's stern he unloaded the beautiful gifts,
the clothing and the gold that Menelaus gave;

162
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:14,100
and urging Telemachus on, he spoke winged words to him:

163
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:17,600
"Aboard with you now — quick —
and order all your companions,

164
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,080
before I reach home and bring the old man the news.

165
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:24,480
For I know this well, in my mind and in my heart:

166
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:27,860
so overpowering is his spirit, he will not let you go —

167
00:11:27,860 --> 00:11:31,460
he will come down here himself
to summon you, and I tell you

168
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:37,040
he will not go back empty-handed.
Angry he will be, whatever happens."

169
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:43,600
So speaking he drove the fine-maned horses back to the
city of the Pylians, and quickly reached the house.

170
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:47,370
And Telemachus called to his
companions and commanded them:

171
00:11:47,370 --> 00:11:50,640
"Set the tackle in order,
companions, through the black ship,

172
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:54,180
and let us board ourselves, and be on our road."

173
00:11:54,180 --> 00:11:57,200
So he spoke, and they listened well and obeyed:

174
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:01,500
and they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks.

175
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:05,820
So he busied himself;
and by the stern of the ship he prayed

176
00:12:05,820 --> 00:12:09,580
and sacrificed to Athena.
And there came up beside him a man

177
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:15,860
from a far country, in flight from Argos for
killing a man — a seer, of the lineage of Melampus,

178
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:19,600
who once, in time past, lived in Pylos mother of flocks,

179
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:22,900
a rich man among the Pylians, in a house of great wealth;

180
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:26,000
but he fled to another people, away from his fatherland

181
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,540
and from great-hearted Neleus, proudest of living men,
who held his vast estate by force a full year round;

182
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:38,480
while Melampus, all that time, in the halls of Phylacus
lay bound in bitter bonds, suffering strong pains

183
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:41,880
for the sake of Neleus' daughter,
and the deep blindness of heart

184
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:45,580
that the goddess laid on him,
the Fury who strikes the house.

185
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:51,460
Yet he escaped his doom, and drove the deep-lowing
cattle from Phylace to Pylos, and paid the ugly deed

186
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,320
of godlike Neleus back, and led the woman home

187
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:58,060
to be his brother's wife.
And he himself went to another people,

188
00:12:58,060 --> 00:13:03,960
to horse-pasturing Argos: there it was fated for
him to live from then on, ruling over many Argives.

189
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:06,340
There he married a wife and built a high-roofed house,

190
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:10,360
and fathered Antiphates and Mantius, strong sons both.

191
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:15,260
Antiphates fathered great-hearted Oicles,
and Oicles Amphiaraus, the rouser of armies,

192
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:17,900
whom Zeus who bears the aegis and Apollo loved

193
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:21,120
in their hearts, with every kind
of love; yet he never reached

194
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:25,520
the threshold of old age:
he died at Thebes — a woman's bribes.

195
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:29,040
And to him were born sons, Alcmaeon and Amphilochus.

196
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:35,380
And Mantius, in his turn, fathered Polypheides and Cleitus —
but Cleitus golden-throned Dawn snatched away,

197
00:13:35,380 --> 00:13:41,320
for his beauty's sake, to live among the immortals;
and of high-hearted Polypheides Apollo made a seer

198
00:13:41,320 --> 00:13:44,880
far the best among mortals, once Amphiaraus was dead.

199
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:50,400
He moved to Hyperesia, angry at his father,
and dwelling there he prophesied for all men.

200
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:56,120
It was this man's son who now came up —
Theoclymenus was his name — and stood beside Telemachus.

201
00:13:56,120 --> 00:13:58,720
He found him pouring wine and praying by his fast

202
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:01,940
black ship, and he raised his
voice and spoke winged words:

203
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:06,020
"Friend — since I find you
burning offerings in this place,

204
00:14:06,020 --> 00:14:09,040
I beg you by these offerings, and by the god, and then

205
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:11,960
by your own head, and your companions' who go with you:

206
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:14,840
tell me truly what I ask; hold nothing back.

207
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:19,000
What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?"

208
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,420
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

209
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:24,300
"Then I will tell you, stranger, and tell you truly.

210
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:30,680
By birth I am of Ithaca; my father is Odysseus —
if he ever was; by now he has died some grim death.

211
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:36,540
That is why I took companions and a black ship
and came out for news of my father, so long gone."

212
00:14:36,540 --> 00:14:38,580
Then godlike Theoclymenus said to him:

213
00:14:38,580 --> 00:14:43,800
"So I too am out of my country: I killed a man of
my own tribe; and his brothers and kinsmen are many

214
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:47,360
in horse-pasturing Argos,
with great power among the Achaeans.

215
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:53,920
It is their death I flee — the black doom of their
hands — since now it is my lot to wander among men.

216
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:56,980
Take me aboard your ship,
since in my flight I have found you

217
00:14:56,980 --> 00:15:01,160
begging: or they will kill me.
I think they are on my track."

218
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:03,440
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

219
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:06,900
"Then I will not push you,
willing as you are, from the trim ship.

220
00:15:06,900 --> 00:15:10,440
Come with us: there you will be
made welcome from what we have."

221
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:16,160
So he spoke, and took the man's bronze spear from him
and laid it flat along the deck of the curved ship;

222
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:21,260
and he himself stepped aboard the seagoing
vessel and sat down in the stern, and beside him

223
00:15:21,260 --> 00:15:25,600
he seated Theoclymenus.
And his men cast off the stern-lines.

224
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:28,840
And Telemachus called to his companions and commanded them

225
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:32,340
to lay hands on the tackle; and they obeyed him swiftly:

226
00:15:32,340 --> 00:15:38,960
they raised the fir mast and stepped it in the
hollow mast-box, made it fast with the forestays,

227
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:43,020
and hauled the white sail up by
the twisted ox-hide halyards.

228
00:15:43,020 --> 00:15:49,380
And gray-eyed Athena sent them a following wind rushing
hard through the clear sky, so the ship might run

229
00:15:49,380 --> 00:15:53,100
her course at speed across the salt water of the sea.

230
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:57,800
And they ran past Crouni,
and Chalcis of the lovely streams.

231
00:15:57,800 --> 00:16:04,360
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark,
and the ship bore on for Pheae, driven by the wind of Zeus,

232
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:07,520
and along bright Elis, where the Epeians hold power.

233
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:10,420
From there again he steered her for the Sharp Islands,

234
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:13,900
wondering whether he would slip past death, or be taken.

235
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:17,280
And in the hut, meanwhile,
Odysseus and the noble swineherd

236
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:20,760
were at supper; and the other
men were at supper with them.

237
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:27,300
When they had put away desire for food and drink,
Odysseus spoke among them, making trial of the swineherd —

238
00:16:27,300 --> 00:16:30,020
whether he would still befriend
him with all care and press him

239
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:33,700
to stay there at the farmstead,
or start him toward the city:

240
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:37,400
"Hear me now, Eumaeus, and all you other companions.

241
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:42,960
At dawn I want to go off to the city and beg there,
so as not to wear you down — you and your men.

242
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:45,620
Only advise me well, and send along a good guide

243
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:49,220
to bring me there. And through
the town I will drift on my own —

244
00:16:49,220 --> 00:16:52,760
I must — and hope someone will hand me a cup and a crust.

245
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:57,940
And I would come to the house of godlike Odysseus
and give my news to watchful-minded Penelope,

246
00:16:57,940 --> 00:17:03,360
and mix with those suitors in their insolence —
they might spare me a dinner out of their endless plenty.

247
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:06,460
I would serve well among them, whatever they wanted done.

248
00:17:06,460 --> 00:17:09,840
For I will tell you this — take it in, and hear me out:

249
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:15,120
by the favor of Hermes the guide, who lends the
grace and glory to all the labor of men's hands,

250
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:17,860
no other mortal can compete with me at service:

251
00:17:17,860 --> 00:17:24,200
at building up a fire, at splitting seasoned wood,
at carving and at roasting meat, at pouring wine —

252
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:26,700
all that lesser men do, waiting on the great."

253
00:17:26,700 --> 00:17:30,420
Then, greatly stirred,
you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

254
00:17:30,420 --> 00:17:36,020
"Ah, stranger — why has such a thought come into
your mind? You must be hungry to die on the spot,

255
00:17:36,020 --> 00:17:42,340
if you truly mean to walk into that crowd of suitors,
whose arrogance and violence reach the iron sky.

256
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:44,340
Their serving-men are nothing like you:

257
00:17:44,340 --> 00:17:50,060
they are young, well-dressed in cloaks and tunics,
sleek their heads always, and handsome their faces —

258
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:55,620
those are the men who wait upon the suitors' polished
tables, loaded down with bread and meat and wine.

259
00:17:55,620 --> 00:18:01,780
No — stay. Nobody is put out by your being here,
not I, and not any of my companions with me.

260
00:18:01,780 --> 00:18:06,600
But when the dear son of Odysseus comes,
that man will clothe you in a cloak and a tunic,

261
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:09,540
and send you wherever your heart and spirit bid you go."

262
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:12,280
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

263
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:14,800
"Eumaeus — may you be as dear to father Zeus

264
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:19,220
as you are to me: for you have stopped
my wandering and my grim misery.

265
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:22,360
Nothing is more wretched for mortal men than roaming:

266
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:25,800
it is for the cursed belly's sake men take hard pains,

267
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:28,940
when wandering and sorrow and grief have come upon them.

268
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:34,320
But now, since you hold me here to wait for that young man,
come, tell me of godlike Odysseus' mother,

269
00:18:34,320 --> 00:18:37,840
and his father, whom he left on the doorsill of old age:

270
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:43,880
are they still living, somewhere under the rays of the sun,
or dead by now, and down in the house of Hades?"

271
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:47,000
Then the swineherd answered him, a leader of men:

272
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,480
"Then I will tell you, stranger, and tell you truly.

273
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:53,000
Laertes is living still, but praying always

274
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,720
to Zeus that the life may waste
out of his limbs in his own halls:

275
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:02,960
so terribly he mourns for his son who is gone,
and for the wise wife of his youth — her death above all

276
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:06,780
has broken him, and brought him
raw old age before his time.

277
00:19:06,780 --> 00:19:14,380
And she died of grief for her son, for her famous son —
a wretched death. May no one die so who lives here

278
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:18,500
and is a friend to me, and has dealt with me in kindness.

279
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:25,520
While she still lived, for all her endless sorrow,
it was some pleasure to me to ask for her and after her,

280
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:32,660
because she reared me herself, with long-robed Ctimene,
her stately daughter, the youngest child she bore.

281
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:36,420
With her I was raised, and honored little less.

282
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:39,780
But when the two of us came to the threshold of our prime,

283
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:43,960
they gave her in marriage to Same —
and got a fortune for her —

284
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:50,080
while me the lady dressed in a mantle and tunic,
fine clothes, and bound good sandals on my feet,

285
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:54,620
and sent me to the farm. But she
loved me from the heart still more.

286
00:19:54,620 --> 00:20:02,060
Now I go without all that. And yet the blessed gods
make the work prosper under my hand, the work I keep to:

287
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:06,120
from it I have eaten and drunk,
and given to those I owe respect.

288
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:09,620
But from the mistress herself
there is nothing sweet to hear,

289
00:20:09,620 --> 00:20:13,560
no word, no kindness,
since that evil fell upon the house —

290
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:20,920
those overweening men. And slaves miss it greatly,
speaking before the mistress, and learning all the news,

291
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:23,720
and eating and drinking, and carrying something afterward

292
00:20:23,720 --> 00:20:28,060
off to the fields — the things that warm a slave's heart."

293
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:31,280
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

294
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:34,780
"Well — how small you must have been, swineherd Eumaeus,

295
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,100
when you were carried so far from
your country and your parents.

296
00:20:38,100 --> 00:20:41,540
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

297
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:47,400
was it a wide-wayed city of men that was sacked,
the one where your father and your honored mother lived,

298
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:50,700
or were you alone with your sheep or with your cattle

299
00:20:50,700 --> 00:20:53,240
when raiders took you into
their ships and carried you over

300
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:56,980
to this man's house, and he paid a worthy price?"

301
00:20:56,980 --> 00:21:00,060
Then the swineherd answered him, a leader of men:

302
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:05,860
"Stranger, since you ask me this, and want it all —
listen in silence now, and take your ease, and sit

303
00:21:05,860 --> 00:21:09,580
drinking your wine. These nights go on past telling.

304
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:13,400
There is time enough for sleep,
and time to take joy in listening;

305
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:17,900
no need to bed down before the hour:
even sleep grows a weariness.

306
00:21:17,900 --> 00:21:21,080
As for the rest of you —
whoever's heart and spirit bid him,

307
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:27,220
go out and sleep; and at the showing of the dawn eat,
and follow out the swine of our master.

308
00:21:27,220 --> 00:21:33,020
But we two in the hut, over our drink and food,
will take our pleasure in each other's grim sorrows,

309
00:21:33,020 --> 00:21:36,860
remembering them. For afterward a
man finds pleasure even in pains,

310
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:39,840
when he has suffered much and wandered far.

311
00:21:39,840 --> 00:21:43,620
So I will tell you what you ask me, what you probe for.

312
00:21:43,620 --> 00:21:46,520
There is an island called Syrie —
you may have heard of it —

313
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:49,340
above Ortygia, where the turnings of the sun are:

314
00:21:49,340 --> 00:21:56,720
not so crowded with people, but a good land,
rich in herds, rich in flocks, full of wine, deep in wheat.

315
00:21:56,720 --> 00:22:02,520
Famine never enters that country, nor does any
hateful sickness fall on wretched mortals there:

316
00:22:02,520 --> 00:22:08,340
but when the generations of men grow old in the town,
Apollo of the silver bow comes with Artemis

317
00:22:08,340 --> 00:22:11,600
and visits them with his gentle arrows, and kills them.

318
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:17,340
and everything is halved between them,
and over both of them my father was the king —

319
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:20,880
Ctesius, son of Ormenus, a match for the immortals.

320
00:22:20,880 --> 00:22:27,240
There Phoenicians came — men famed for ships, gnawers,
with countless trinkets in their black vessel.

321
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:33,340
Now in my father's house there was a Phoenician woman,
beautiful and tall, and skilled in glorious handwork;

322
00:22:33,340 --> 00:22:37,140
and the Phoenicians, subtle-minded, led her astray.

323
00:22:37,140 --> 00:22:42,420
Washing clothes by the hollow ship, one of them first
lay with her in love — the thing that leads astray

324
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:45,740
the minds of women, even one whose works are honest.

325
00:22:45,740 --> 00:22:49,080
Then he asked her who she was, and where she came from,

326
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:52,480
and she pointed at once to my father's high-roofed house:

327
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:58,400
'I claim my birth from Sidon, rich in bronze,
and I am the daughter of Arybas, a man of flowing wealth.

328
00:22:58,400 --> 00:23:04,760
But Taphian pirates snatched me as I came in from
the fields, and carried me over the sea and sold me

329
00:23:04,760 --> 00:23:08,740
to this man's house; and he paid a worthy price.'

330
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:11,780
Then the man who had lain with her in secret said:

331
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:14,700
'Then would you come home again with us, to look on

332
00:23:14,700 --> 00:23:18,160
the high-roofed house of your
father and mother — and on them?

333
00:23:18,160 --> 00:23:21,460
For they are living still, and are called wealthy.'

334
00:23:21,460 --> 00:23:23,240
Then the woman answered him and said:

335
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:29,420
'Even that could be, if you sailors were willing to
bind yourselves by oath to bring me home unharmed.'

336
00:23:29,420 --> 00:23:33,420
So she spoke, and they all swore the oath she demanded.

337
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:38,740
And when they had sworn and sealed the oath entire,
the woman spoke among them again and answered:

338
00:23:38,740 --> 00:23:44,780
'Silence, now. Let none of your company say a word
to me, meeting me in the street or at the well,

339
00:23:44,780 --> 00:23:50,400
or someone may go to the house and tell the old man;
and he, suspecting, would bind me in hard bonds,

340
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:53,040
and set his mind on death for all of you.

341
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:56,240
Keep the word in your hearts,
and press the buying of your cargo.

342
00:23:56,240 --> 00:24:01,720
But when your ship is full and freighted with goods,
let a message come quickly to me at the house.

343
00:24:01,720 --> 00:24:05,580
For I will bring gold too — whatever comes under my hand.

344
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:08,720
And there is another thing I
would gladly give for my passage:

345
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:15,780
I nurse a child of this fine man in the halls —
such a knowing little thing, always running out beside me.

346
00:24:15,780 --> 00:24:18,560
I would bring him aboard the ship, and he would fetch you

347
00:24:18,560 --> 00:24:23,540
a boundless price, wherever you
sell him among strange-tongued men.'

348
00:24:23,540 --> 00:24:26,920
So speaking, she went off to the beautiful house.

349
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:32,300
And they stayed among us a whole year long,
and bought and gathered goods in their hollow ship;

350
00:24:32,300 --> 00:24:37,960
When the hollow hull was freighted for their crossing,
they sent a messenger up to bring the woman word.

351
00:24:37,960 --> 00:24:43,780
A man came, deep in cunning, to my father's house with
a necklace of gold, strung here and there with amber.

352
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:45,940
And in the hall the slave-women and my honored mother

353
00:24:45,940 --> 00:24:49,700
kept turning it in their hands
and drinking it with their eyes,

354
00:24:49,700 --> 00:24:53,900
offering him their price;
and he nodded to the woman in silence.

355
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:56,480
He nodded — then went his way to the hollow ship;

356
00:24:56,480 --> 00:24:59,480
and she took me by the hand and
led me out through the doors.

357
00:24:59,480 --> 00:25:05,260
In the forecourt she found the cups and the tables
of the banqueters, the men who attended on my father:

358
00:25:05,260 --> 00:25:08,360
they had gone out to the people's session and debate.

359
00:25:08,360 --> 00:25:10,480
Quickly she hid three goblets away at her breast

360
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:14,880
and carried them off. And I followed —
in my child's witlessness.

361
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:19,740
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark,
and we came at a run to the famous harbor,

362
00:25:19,740 --> 00:25:23,800
where the sea-swift ship of the Phoenician men was lying.

363
00:25:23,800 --> 00:25:26,600
Then they embarked, and went
their way over the watery paths,

364
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:30,760
with the two of us aboard; and Zeus sent down a wind.

365
00:25:30,760 --> 00:25:37,400
Six days we sailed on, alike by day and night;
but when Zeus, the son of Cronos, brought the seventh day,

366
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:39,920
then Artemis the shooter of arrows struck the woman,

367
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:44,580
and she dropped with a thud
into the bilge, like a sea-tern.

368
00:25:44,580 --> 00:25:50,240
spoil for the seals and fishes,
and I was left alone, my heart aching.

369
00:25:50,240 --> 00:25:56,860
Then wind and water carried them along to Ithaca,
and there Laertes bought me, with his own possessions.

370
00:25:56,860 --> 00:26:00,100
So it was my eyes first looked upon this land."

371
00:26:00,100 --> 00:26:03,660
Then Odysseus, seed of Zeus, answered him with words:

372
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:06,740
"Eumaeus, you have deeply stirred
the heart here in my breast,

373
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:10,160
telling all these things —
the sorrows your heart has suffered.

374
00:26:10,160 --> 00:26:14,140
And yet, I say, Zeus set the good beside the evil for you,

375
00:26:14,140 --> 00:26:16,680
since after all that pain you came to the house of a kind

376
00:26:16,680 --> 00:26:22,420
man, who provides your food and drink with every care,
and you live a good life — while I come to you here

377
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:26,440
as I am: still wandering through the many cities of men."

378
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:32,560
So they spoke to each other of such things,
then lay down to sleep — no long time, a little only,

379
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:38,860
for soon Dawn came on her fair throne. And on the
shore the companions of Telemachus were striking sail:

380
00:26:38,860 --> 00:26:42,320
they took the mast down smartly
and rowed her in to anchorage,

381
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:46,620
and threw the mooring-stones out,
and made the stern-lines fast;

382
00:26:46,620 --> 00:26:52,880
and they themselves stepped out into the breaking
surf and readied a meal, and mixed the sparkling wine.

383
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:58,700
And when they had put away desire for food and drink,
clear-headed Telemachus began and spoke among them:

384
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:02,180
"Row the black ship on now, all of you, to the city.

385
00:27:02,180 --> 00:27:04,900
I will make my way to the fields and to the herdsmen,

386
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:09,680
and at evening, once I have looked
over the farmlands, I will come down.

387
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:13,020
In the morning I will set before
you the wages of the voyage:

388
00:27:13,020 --> 00:27:16,540
a good feast of meats, and honey-hearted wine."

389
00:27:16,540 --> 00:27:19,000
Then godlike Theoclymenus said to him:

390
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:25,180
"And where shall I go, dear child? Whose house shall
I come to, of the men who hold power in rocky Ithaca?

391
00:27:25,180 --> 00:27:29,000
Straight to your mother's house —
is that where I go — and yours?"

392
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,600
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

393
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:34,980
"At any other time I would tell you: come to our house —

394
00:27:34,980 --> 00:27:38,720
there is no lack there of what a
guest is given. But for you, now,

395
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:42,080
it would be the worse way: I will be gone, and my mother

396
00:27:42,080 --> 00:27:45,360
will not see you. For not often
before the suitors in that house

397
00:27:45,360 --> 00:27:50,100
does she appear: apart from them
she weaves at her loom, upstairs.

398
00:27:50,100 --> 00:27:52,520
But I will name you another man you might go to:

399
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:57,980
Eurymachus, glorious son of thoughtful Polybus,
whom now the men of Ithaca look on like a god.

400
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:00,780
For he is far the best man there — and hungriest of all

401
00:28:00,780 --> 00:28:04,180
to marry my mother and take the honor that was Odysseus'.

402
00:28:04,180 --> 00:28:07,680
But Zeus the Olympian, who has his home in the bright sky,

403
00:28:07,680 --> 00:28:11,800
knows whether before any wedding
he will bring their evil day."

404
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:15,180
And even as he spoke, a bird flew by on his right hand:

405
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:18,400
a hawk, the swift messenger of Apollo. In its talons

406
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:21,360
it held a dove, and was tearing it,
and shook the feathers down

407
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:25,120
to the ground, midway between
the ship and Telemachus himself.

408
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:28,040
And Theoclymenus called him apart from his companions,

409
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:31,720
and pressed his hand, and spoke a word, and named him:

410
00:28:31,720 --> 00:28:35,080
"Telemachus, not without a god did
that bird fly past on the right:

411
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:39,180
I knew it, looking at it face-on, for a bird of omen.

412
00:28:39,180 --> 00:28:44,680
There is no line more kingly than your own in the
land of Ithaca. No: yours is the power forever."

413
00:28:44,680 --> 00:28:47,300
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

414
00:28:47,300 --> 00:28:50,380
"Ah, stranger, if only that word might be accomplished:

415
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:52,820
then you would quickly know my friendship, and gifts

416
00:28:52,820 --> 00:28:56,540
and plenty for me — any man who
met you would call you blessed."

417
00:28:56,540 --> 00:29:00,360
And he turned and spoke to Piraeus, his trusted companion:

418
00:29:00,360 --> 00:29:03,560
"Piraeus, son of Clytius — you who in other things obey me

419
00:29:03,560 --> 00:29:06,600
most of all the companions who followed me to Pylos:

420
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:13,140
now too, I ask you, take this stranger to your house
and care for him kindly, and honor him, until I come."

421
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:15,940
Then Piraeus, famous for the spear, answered him:

422
00:29:15,940 --> 00:29:19,080
"Telemachus, even if you should
stay out there a long while,

423
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:22,480
I will look after this man; no guest's due shall he lack."

424
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:25,500
So speaking, he went aboard the ship,
and called the companions

425
00:29:25,500 --> 00:29:29,120
to come aboard themselves and loose the stern-lines;

426
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:32,940
and they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks.

427
00:29:32,940 --> 00:29:36,220
And Telemachus bound his beautiful sandals under his feet

428
00:29:36,220 --> 00:29:39,060
and took his strong spear up from the deck of the ship,

429
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:43,340
sharp-tipped with bronze.
And the men cast off the stern-lines:

430
00:29:43,340 --> 00:29:50,100
they shoved out and sailed on to the city, as they were
ordered by Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus.

431
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:54,080
But his feet carried him swiftly,
striding, until he reached the yard

432
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:57,660
where his swine past counting were;
and beside them the swineherd

433
00:29:57,660 --> 00:30:01,872
slept, a good man, whose heart was loyal to his masters.
